<p>Russia said Thursday its forces would remain in Mali to support the country’s military authorities and fight extremism, rejecting a call by Tuareg separatists for Moscow to withdraw after recent rebel attacks. The statement followed a weekend offensive in which separatist rebels and jihadist fighters attacked cities and military bases, forced Russian troops from the northern town of Kidal, and killed Mali’s defence minister, several publications reported. Other reports said the attacks also hit the capital and that thousands gathered in Bamako for the defence minister’s funeral.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/hHhsCSb_PP3M4wuo--tyk9zhnoI7rR3A_oKbz5OwVBPTCz9KFzfPFzjcoSXj1EQMgsEhM4ozAGdZlm_dQypp4UZ1S-DwX_60AGKRIkswdPbEVQ78HmljuJISkwzI0bAjRxa-mkoQ2qZe6wu9wpYPepb5Bw5t0zj-Tilt2ThJnYWRS_hksJLyGFU-KW8Eoj6AV6v6XFpqOAhV-iWo-1NynbSr4u9uPCYQ9QqlMGVEYwohgFa5rAemvXBtFVGCGefvm4lAYheDEim8dirQDQ' alt='A column of black smoke rises above buildings as traffic passes the Africa Tower monument in Bamako, Mali on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Russian deployment: About 2,000 Russian soldiers are stationed in Mali, according to Iltalehti, which cited the scale of Moscow’s presence after the weekend attacks.</li><li>Attack scale: Businessam described the weekend attacks as the largest against Mali’s military junta in 15 years.</li><li>Named factions: Eestieest said Russia’s Africa Corps was helping Mali’s armed forces repel an onslaught by the Azawad Liberation Front (FLA) and its Jamaat Nusrat al-Islam wal-Muslimin (JNIM) allies.</li><li>Minister detail: Kathimerini reported that Mali’s slain defence minister trained in Russia and was killed in a suicide attack.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NRC: Russia’s retreat is bringing Mali more uncertainty, NRC wrote. (<a href='https://www.nrc.nl/nieuws/2026/04/30/aftocht-russen-brengt-mali-vooral-nog-meer-onzekerheid-a4926760'>NRC</a>)</li><li>Financial Times: Losses suffered by Wagner Group’s successor in Mali ca